.TH LOSTC 1 "2026-08-01" "lost-commander 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lostc \- dual-pane terminal file manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lostc
.RI [ LEFT_DIR ]
.RI [ RIGHT_DIR ]
.br
.B lostc
.B \-\-list
.RI [ DIR ]
.br
.B lostc
.RB [ \-\-help | \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lostc
is a file manager in the Norton Commander tradition: two directory panels
side by side, function keys along the bottom, and a command line under the
panels. Everything it does to the filesystem is shared with
.BR lostc\-gui (1),
which is the same program drawn in a window.
.PP
With no arguments both panels open on the current directory. One argument
sets the left panel, two set both.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-list " " [\fIDIR\fR]
Print what a panel would show for \fIDIR\fR and exit. The whole listing
pipeline with no terminal attached, which makes it useful for scripting and
for checking that a build works.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a summary of the keys and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B Tab
The other panel.
.TP
.B Enter
Open: descend into a directory, walk into an archive, or hand a file to the
desktop.
.TP
.B Backspace
The parent directory, when nothing is typed on the command line.
.TP
.BR F1 - F10
Help, rename, view, edit, copy, move, mkdir, delete, sort, quit. Letters go
to the command line, so
.B q
does not quit.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-Q
Quit. There is a second way out because a terminal may keep
.B F10
for its own menu and never pass it on. GTK binds it to the menubar for every
application, so GNOME Terminal, Terminator and Tilix take it by default: the
keystroke is consumed above the pseudo\-terminal and nothing running inside
can see it. Worse, the menubar then has the keyboard, and a
.B q
there is File \(-> Quit \- which closes the terminal and every tab in it.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-C
Cancel a running copy or move; with nothing running, clear the command line;
with neither, quit \- which is what the keystroke means everywhere else in a
terminal.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-Z
Suspend, giving the terminal back to the shell;
.B fg
resumes. Windows has no job control, so there it does nothing.
.TP
.B Space
Mark the file under the cursor, when nothing is typed.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-H
Show or hide hidden files.
.TP
.B Alt\-T
The directory tree, above the files. \fBEnter\fR opens a directory and drops
into its files; \fBEscape\fR climbs back to the tree.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-J
The journal: an account of what has been done.
.SH COMMAND LINE
Typing goes to the command line under the panels, and \fBEnter\fR runs it in
the directory the panel is showing. The program hands the terminal over while
it runs, so interactive commands, colour and pagers all work, and waits for a
key before taking the screen back.
.PP
An empty command line means the panels: \fBSpace\fR marks, \fBBackspace\fR
climbs, \fBEscape\fR returns to the tree. Once something is typed they are
characters.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/lost\-commander/settings.toml
Settings: theme, chosen shell, window arrangement.
.TP
.I ~/.config/lost\-commander/bookmarks.toml
Saved local and network locations.
.TP
.I ~/.config/lost\-commander/journal/
The account of what was done, one file a day.
.PP
On macOS these are under
.IR ~/Library/Application\ Support/lost\-commander/ ,
and on Windows under
.IR %APPDATA%\\lost\-commander\\ .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B EDITOR
.TQ
.B VISUAL
What \fBF4\fR opens a file with. \fBVISUAL\fR wins.
.TP
.B SHELL
The shell used for the command line, on Unix.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on a clean quit, non-zero if the terminal could not be set up.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lostc\-gui (1),
.BR mc (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Alexander Garmash.
.SH BUGS
Report at https://github.com/GarAlex/lost-commander/issues
